From: ginux-watch-agent <actions@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 09:22:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tests(devices): pin Random read xorshift64 sequence and state
 advancement

### What
Pins `DeviceRegistry::read` Random xorshift64 sequence: first four bytes match the reference algorithm from default seed, and successive reads advance state.

### Why it matters
The PRNG drives /dev/random output; replacing XOR with OR or flipping shift direction produces a degenerate or predictable stream. Existing tests verify read succeeds but never pin output values.

### Mutation evidence (fail-then-pass)
| Mutant | Existing suite | New test |
|---|---|---|
| `^= ... << 13` -> `\|= ... << 13` | PASS (survives) | FAIL (killed) |
| `<< 13` -> `>> 13` | PASS (survives) | FAIL (killed) |

Baseline: PASS. Reproduce: `cargo test -p ginux-devices --lib random_read_pins`.

Spec-Ref: xorshift64 PRNG (Marsaglia 2003), /dev/random semantics

Prepared with AI assistance; reviewed by me.
---
 crates/ginux-devices/src/lib.rs | 22 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+)

diff --git a/crates/ginux-devices/src/lib.rs b/crates/ginux-devices/src/lib.rs
index 2a3b9f7a..3a340226 100644
--- a/crates/ginux-devices/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/ginux-devices/src/lib.rs
@@ -943,4 +943,26 @@ mod tests {
         // foreign instance cannot reach the descriptor
         assert!(registry.write(2, null, b"abc").is_err());
     }
+
+    #[test]
+    fn random_read_pins_xorshift_sequence() {
+        let mut registry: DeviceRegistry<8, 8> = DeviceRegistry::new();
+        registry
+            .assign(
+                1,
+                10,
+                DeviceName::new(b"random").unwrap(),
+                DeviceKind::Random,
+            )
+            .unwrap();
+        let fd = registry.open(1, b"random").unwrap();
+        let mut buf = [0u8; 4];
+        registry.read(1, fd, &mut buf).unwrap();
+        // pin first four bytes of xorshift64 from actual default seed 0x6a09_e667_f3bc_c909
+        assert_eq!(buf, [0xdb, 0xbe, 0xcd, 0xea]);
+        // second read continues the sequence (state persists)
+        let mut buf2 = [0u8; 4];
+        registry.read(1, fd, &mut buf2).unwrap();
+        assert_ne!(buf, buf2);
+    }
 }
